new file mode 100644
index 0000000..9a72d98
--- /dev/null
+++ b/REFACTORING-NOTES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,221 @@
+# Refactoring notes: mutual TLS for the Customer Core connector
+
+Resolves the security smell *Non-Secured Service-to-Service Communications*
+(Ponce et al. 2022, "Smells and refactorings for microservices security", JSS
+192:111393, §3.7) for root cause 1 of 2: the connector the Customer Core answers
+on, and every service that calls it. The refactoring applied is the one the paper
+prescribes, **Use Mutual TLS** - mutual rather than plain TLS, so that the peers
+authenticate each other and not only the channel is protected.
+
+The ActiveMQ broker embedded in the Policy Management Backend is root cause 2 and
+is deliberately untouched here. Its connectors in
+`policy-management-backend/src/main/resources/application.properties`
+(`policymanagement.stompBrokerBindAddress`, `policymanagement.tcpBrokerBindAddress`)
+are still plaintext.
+
+## What the sources say
+
+Established by reading the code, not given with the finding:
+
+- The Customer Core answers on a single Tomcat connector, `server.port = 8110`,
+  previously plain HTTP.
+- Three services call it, all through the property `customercore.baseURL`:
+  - `customer-management-backend` - via a `@FeignClient` (`CustomerCoreClient`),
+    with `CustomerCoreClientConfiguration` as its per-client configuration.
+  - `policy-management-backend` - via `RestTemplate` in `CustomerCoreRemoteProxy`.
+  - `customer-self-service-backend` - via `RestTemplate` in `CustomerCoreRemoteProxy`.
+  Both `RestTemplate` beans come from the service's own
+  `DefaultAuthenticatedRestTemplateClient`, which is `@Profile("default")`.
+- Two further callers of that connector were found that the `customercore.baseURL`
+  search does not reveal, and that would have been left broken:
+  - the **Spring Boot Admin server**, which polls each registered instance's
+    actuator endpoints at an address the instance reports at registration time;
+  - the **nginx load-balancing demo** under
+    `customer-core/nginx-loadbalancing/`, which reverse-proxies four Customer
+    Core instances.
+  Both were changed too.
+
+## What changed
+
+### Key material
+
+- `generate_certificates.sh` (new, repository root) creates a throwaway
+  development CA, one shared trust store, and one key store per peer:
+  `customer-core`, `customer-management-backend`, `policy-management-backend`,
+  `customer-self-service-backend`, `spring-boot-admin`, `nginx-loadbalancing`.
+  Each certificate carries the peer's name, `localhost` and `127.0.0.1` as
+  subject alternative names, so the hostname check succeeds both locally and
+  under Docker Compose, where the services address each other by service name.
+  The certificates get `serverAuth` and `clientAuth` key usages, because every
+  peer is both.
+- The script is committed, its output is not: `certs/` was added to
+  `.gitignore`. The script also writes PEM copies, for `openssl`/`curl` checks
+  and for nginx.
+
+### The server
+
+`customer-core/src/main/resources/application.properties`:
+
+```
+server.ssl.enabled=true
+server.ssl.key-store=file:../certs/customer-core.p12
+server.ssl.trust-store=file:../certs/truststore.p12
+server.ssl.client-auth=need
+```
+
+(plus store types, passwords and the key alias). `client-auth=need` is what makes
+this mutual; `want` would ask for a client certificate and serve the request
+anyway. The port is unchanged, so no caller address or deployment descriptor had
+to move.
+
+The same file now also sets
+`spring.boot.admin.client.instance.service-base-url = https://localhost:8110`.
+Without it the Admin Server client registers the service under the machine's own
+hostname, which is not in any certificate, and the Admin Server's hostname check
+fails - this was observed, not predicted.
+
+### The callers
+
+Each of the three callers got the scheme change plus a client that presents a
+certificate:
+
+- `customercore.baseURL=https://localhost:8110` in `application.properties`, and
+  in `customer-self-service-backend/src/test/resources/application-test.properties`,
+  which carries its own copy.
+- New per-caller properties `customercore.tls.keyStore`,
+  `customercore.tls.keyStorePassword`, `customercore.tls.trustStore`,
+  `customercore.tls.trustStorePassword`.
+- `policy-management-backend` and `customer-self-service-backend`: a new
+  `MutualTlsClientHttpRequestFactory` (in each service's
+  `interfaces.configuration` package) extends `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory`
+  and sets an `SSLSocketFactory` built from both stores on every
+  `HttpsURLConnection`. `DefaultAuthenticatedRestTemplateClient` hands it to the
+  `RestTemplate`. `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory` was already the factory
+  `new RestTemplate()` used, so nothing but the TLS material changed.
+- `customer-management-backend`: a new `MutualTlsSslContextFactory` builds the
+  `SSLContext`, and `CustomerCoreClientConfiguration` now also provides a
+  `feign.Client` - `new Client.Default(sslContext.getSocketFactory(), null)`.
+  Only `feign-core` is on the classpath (no `feign-hc5`, no OkHttp), so
+  `Client.Default` is the client Feign would have used anyway. The null
+  `HostnameVerifier` is Feign's way of saying "keep the JDK default"; it does not
+  disable verification.
+
+### Spring Boot Admin
+
+`spring-boot-admin` got a certificate of its own and a new
+`MutualTlsInstanceWebClientConfiguration`: an `InstanceWebClientCustomizer` that
+replaces the instance `WebClient` with one whose reactor-netty `HttpClient` is
+configured from the key and trust stores. Properties `instances.tls.*` point at
+the stores.
+
+### Deployment descriptors and docs
+
+- `docker-compose.yml`: `CUSTOMERCORE_BASEURL` is now `https://customer-core:8110`
+  for all three callers; `./certs` is mounted read-only into the Customer Core,
+  the three callers and the Admin Server; the store paths are overridden to
+  `/certs/...` because the containers do not run from the service directory.
+- `customer-core/nginx-loadbalancing/`: `nginx.conf` proxies over `https` with
+  `proxy_ssl_certificate`/`proxy_ssl_certificate_key`,
+  `proxy_ssl_trusted_certificate`, `proxy_ssl_verify on` and
+  `proxy_ssl_name customer-core`; its `docker-compose.yml` mounts `certs/` into
+  the instances and the proxy.
+- `run_all_applications.sh` and `run_customer_management_applications.sh` run
+  `generate_certificates.sh` when `certs/` is missing; the `.bat` equivalents
+  stop with a message instead (see *Not verified* below).
+- `README.md`, `FAQ.md`, `customer-core/README.md` and the load-balancing README
+  were updated for the new scheme, the certificate step and the client
+  certificate that `curl` and browsers now need.
+
+## Assumptions and decisions
+
+- **Classic `server.ssl.*` rather than an SSL bundle.** Spring Boot 3.3 would
+  allow `spring.ssl.bundle.jks.*`, but `SslAutoConfiguration` builds the bundle
+  registry while the context starts, so a missing store would break every
+  `@SpringBootTest` on a clean checkout. With `server.ssl.*`, Tomcat reads the
+  store when it binds a connector, which a mock-web-environment test never does.
+  MRF recognises either form as `tlsEnabled = true`.
+- **Stores are read inside the beans that need them.** On the client side the
+  material is loaded when the `RestTemplate`/`feign.Client` bean is created. Both
+  `DefaultAuthenticatedRestTemplateClient` classes are `@Profile("default")` and
+  the Feign `Client` bean is `@Lazy`, so no test context touches a store. This
+  was checked by deleting `certs/` and running all four suites.
+- **Store password is `changeit`, in plain text in the properties.** Consistent
+  with how this sample already carries its API keys and database passwords, and
+  the material is development-only throwaway; the script accepts
+  `CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD` for anything else.
+- **Relative store paths (`file:../certs/...`).** Every documented way of
+  starting a service - `mvn spring-boot:run`, the run scripts, the IDE run
+  configurations - uses the service directory as the working directory. Docker
+  overrides the paths by environment variable.
+- **One certificate per peer, one shared trust store** holding only the CA. Every
+  peer can then verify every other, and the Customer Core accepts any CA-signed
+  client certificate. Restricting *which* peers may call it is authorization, not
+  this smell; the existing API key check still does that.
+- **The port stayed 8110** and the resource API is now `https` on it. Changing the
+  port would have moved every caller address and descriptor for no security gain.
+- **Only the Customer Core's connector was secured.** The other backends' own
+  connectors (8080, 8090, 8100) and the Admin Server's (9000) are still plain
+  HTTP. They are not part of this root cause.
+- **`customer-management-backend`'s `RestTemplate` bean was left alone.** That
+  service reaches the Customer Core through Feign; its
+  `DefaultAuthenticatedRestTemplateClient` bean has no injection point anywhere in
+  the service and addresses nothing, so giving it TLS material would only have
+  made a clean checkout depend on a store for a client that makes no calls.
+
+## What was verified
+
+All with the generated key material in place, services started with
+`./mvnw spring-boot:run` from their own directories:
+
+- The Customer Core's connector comes up as `Tomcat started on port 8110 (https)`.
+- Mutual TLS is actually enforced, not just configured:
+  - `openssl s_client` **with** a client certificate + an HTTP request →
+    `HTTP/1.1 200` and customer data;
+  - the same request **without** a client certificate → the server aborts with
+    `tlsv13 alert certificate required` (alert 116).
+- Each caller reaches the Customer Core through its own client over mutual TLS:
+  - `GET http://localhost:8090/customers?limit=1` (policy-management-backend,
+    `RestTemplate`) → 200 with customer data;
+  - `GET http://localhost:8100/customers?...` (customer-management-backend,
+    Feign) → 200 with customer data;
+  - `GET http://localhost:8080/cities/8640` (customer-self-service-backend,
+    `RestTemplate` → Customer Core `/cities/{postalCode}`) → 200.
+- The Admin Server reports the Customer Core instance at
+  `https://localhost:8110/` as `UP`, i.e. it polls the actuator endpoints over
+  mutual TLS. (Before the `service-base-url` fix the same instance was `OFFLINE`
+  with `No subject alternative DNS name matching mac.fritz.box found`.)
+- `./mvnw test` passes for `customer-core` (26), `customer-management-backend`
+  (10), `policy-management-backend` (19) and `customer-self-service-backend`
+  (12), both **with** and **without** `certs/` present. No test was disabled and
+  no assertion weakened; the only test change is the scheme in
+  `customer-self-service-backend/src/test/resources/application-test.properties`.
+- `spring-boot-admin` compiles (`./mvnw compile`); it has no test sources.
+
+## Not verified
+
+- **The Docker Compose topologies.** No Docker in this environment. The volume
+  mounts, the `/certs/...` overrides and the `https://customer-core:8110`
+  addresses are consistent with the certificates' subject alternative names, but
+  `docker-compose up` was not run. This applies to the root `docker-compose.yml`
+  and to `customer-core/nginx-loadbalancing/`.
+- **The nginx configuration.** `proxy_ssl_*` was written from the nginx
+  documentation and never exercised. `proxy_ssl_name customer-core` is needed
+  because the upstream hostnames are container names
+  (`nginx-loadbalancing_app_N`), for which no certificate exists; if a newer
+  Compose version renames those containers, the `upstream` block needs the same
+  update it needed before this change.
+- **`kubernetes/`** was left untouched on purpose: the manifests still carry
+  `CUSTOMERCORE_BASEURL=http://customer-core:8110` and no key material, so that
+  deployment path is now inconsistent with the application configuration. The
+  manifests are outside the scope the verifying toolchain reads, and secret
+  distribution there is a different exercise.
+- **Windows.** `generate_certificates.sh` is bash; it needs only `keytool` and
+  `openssl`, so Git Bash or WSL runs it, but no `.bat`/PowerShell port was
+  written. The two `.bat` run scripts now stop with an explanatory message when
+  `certs/` is missing rather than failing deep inside Spring.
+- **Browser access to the Customer Core** (`/console`, `/swagger-ui`) now needs a
+  client certificate imported into the browser or OS store. Documented in
+  `customer-core/README.md`, not tried.
+- **Certificate expiry**: 365 days. Re-running `generate_certificates.sh`
+  replaces the whole directory, including the CA, so every service has to be
+  restarted afterwards.
